# piston local variables (from the piston's define section). Declared values
# are the defaults; anything the piston has since set is restored from the
# persisted state entity so variables survive HA restarts and recompiles —
# webCoRE keeps piston variables in state, so this matches it.
pv = {{ variables }}

# ORDER MATTERS (fixed 2026-07-30): the restore used to run ABOVE this line,
# so it wrote into a `pv` that did not exist yet — a NameError swallowed by
# the except, logged as a warning nobody reads — and then `pv = ...` would
# have overwritten it anyway. Persisted variables never survived a restart.
# Defaults first, saved values overlaid on top.
try:
    _saved = state.getattr("pyscript.{{ unique_name }}_state") or {}
    for _k, _v in (_saved.get("vars") or {}).items():
        pv[_k] = _v
except Exception as _e:
    log.warning(f"[{{ piston_id }}] could not restore variables: {_e}")
# global variable values, snapshotted at compile time (recompile refreshes).
# ALWAYS defined, even when empty (2026-07-30): _gv() in the runtime falls
# back to `gv.get(name)` whenever the persisted pyscript entity is missing —
# which is the NORMAL path on a fresh deploy, before any piston has written
# that global. Emitting `gv` conditionally meant an always-present helper
# depended on a sometimes-present variable; consistent today only by
# accident of how used_globals is collected. An empty dict costs nothing and
# turns a possible NameError into a missing key.
gv = {{ global_values if global_values is defined and global_values else {} }}
